# Qualification evidence is attested by sha256 digests recorded inside the
# evidence trees. Any content filter (EOL normalization included) silently
# breaks the attestation on checkout, so evidence bytes must round-trip git
# exactly.
qualification/** -text
build/out/** -text

# Image content. These files are copied verbatim into the image by
# build/scripts/install-root.py and are then parsed by systemd, systemd-tmpfiles
# and the shell, none of which treat a trailing CR as whitespace: a unit
# checked out with CRLF yields directive values ending in \r, and a tmpfiles
# line likewise. A Windows checkout under core.autocrlf=true produces exactly
# that, and the corruption would reach the image only when someone built from
# that working tree — silently, and nowhere near the checkout that caused it.
systemd/** -text
config/** -text
*.sh -text

# Byte-attested evidence must bypass content filters.
#
# release/evidence.py verifies each record by hashing the file at its
# evidenceReference and comparing against the recorded contentDigest. Any
# checkout-time filter changes those bytes, so a record generated on one
# platform stops verifying on another. That is not theoretical: the digest
# recorded for hardware-evidence.json below matches this file's CRLF bytes on a
# Windows checkout and not its committed bytes.
#
# These are listed one path at a time rather than as operations/data/** so that
# the rule covers exactly what is attested. A new attested file needs a new line
# here, and tests/evidence/test_byte_roundtrip.py fails until it gets one.
evidence/build/beta-archive-digest.txt          -text
evidence/build/beta-license-scan.log            -text
evidence/vulnerability/beta-grype.json          -text
operations/data/hardware-evidence.json          -text
operations/data/pilot-requirements.json         -text
operations/data/qualification-matrices.json     -text
operations/data/signing-keys.json               -text

# Character package assets are attested by the package's own manifest.
#
# companion/character/package.py verifies every declared asset against the
# sizeBytes and sha256 in manifest.json before a renderer may touch it, and
# refuses the whole package on a mismatch. A checkout-time EOL filter changes
# those bytes, so a package that verifies on the machine that built it stops
# verifying everywhere else.
#
# Measured, not anticipated: LICENSE.txt is 308 bytes committed and 314 bytes
# in a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true, and the built-in character —
# which §16 requires to pass the same validator as an imported one — failed its
# own integrity check in 23 tests. The PNGs were unaffected because git already
# treats them as binary; it is the one text file in the package that broke it.
assets/companion/characters/** -text
